# .punused-ignore — adjudicated punused findings.
#
# Format: one literal substring per line, matched against a punused finding
# line ("<file>:<line>:<col> <kind> <name> is <verdict> (EUxxxx)"). Full-line
# "#" comments carry the reason. Patterns deliberately omit the file:line:col
# prefix: an unrelated edit above a symbol shifts its line number, which would
# silently stale the entry and fail the gate on a finding already adjudicated.
#
# What punused (github.com/bep/punused) can and cannot see, since every entry
# below turns on it: it asks gopls for the references of each exported symbol
# declared in a NON-test file (symbols declared in _test.go files are never
# reported, and gopls is asked with IncludeDeclaration:false), then reports
# EU1002 when there are no references at all and EU1001 when every reference
# sits in a _test.go file. Its README names the blind spots — it does not see
# references via reflect, and interfaces produce surprises — and its own golden
# test shows an interface method reported unused while an implementation of it
# is being called. Each entry names which blind spot, or which structural
# constraint, applies.
#
# Not in this file, because they were resolved instead: api.UserInfo.Token and
# plex.Show.RatingKey (deleted, no production reader), streams.StreamTypeVideo
# (deleted from production; the wire value now lives in the test that needs a
# third stream kind), plex.NewClientFromHTTP (moved to
# internal/testsupport/plexclient).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# json.Unmarshaler reached reflectively by encoding/json.
# Blind spot: "It does not detect references via reflect."
#
# streams.FlexInt is the Plex number-or-quoted-number wire primitive. Nothing
# calls UnmarshalJSON by name; encoding/json finds it through the
# json.Unmarshaler interface while decoding the production structs that embed
# FlexInt — streams.Episode.Index and .ParentIndex
# (internal/streams/streams.go:41-42), plex.HistoryItem.AccountID and .ViewedAt
# (internal/plex/types.go:74,78). Those decode on the production fetch path
# (internal/plex/library.go, fetchEpisodeList) and production
# reads the results: Episode.SeasonNum/Num (streams.go:47-58) feed the
# season/episode comparison in internal/tracksync/tracks.go:465-471. Delete the
# method and every quoted Plex index ("14") starts failing to decode.
method (*FlexInt).UnmarshalJSON is used in test only

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# internal/testsupport/** — fixture packages.
# Structural: every export here is EU1001 by construction, not by accident.
#
# These are ordinary .go files, so punused enumerates their symbols, but every
# legitimate consumer is a _test.go file in a DIFFERENT package, so every
# reference is test-only. Ordinary .go files are not a mistake: a _test.go file
# is visible only to its own package's test binary, so a fixture shared across
# packages cannot live in one. Entries are per-symbol rather than one
# directory-wide pattern, so a fixture that becomes genuinely unreferenced
# still fails the gate.

# fakeapi.NewCache — the in-memory api.Cache fake. 113 call sites across three
# other packages' tests (internal/tracksync, internal/deepscan, internal/users).
function NewCache is used in test only

# fakeapi.RunCacheContract — the shared api.Cache contract, run against BOTH
# implementations: the REAL cache at internal/cache/cache_test.go:554
# (RunCacheContract(t, New())) and the fake at
# internal/testsupport/fakeapi/cache_contract_test.go:7. Deleting it deletes
# the production cache's contract coverage, not just the fake's.
function RunCacheContract is used in test only

# fakeapi.(*Plex).CallNames — reader for the fake's ordered call log; 73
# assertion sites in internal/tracksync and internal/deepscan tests pin "the pass
# made exactly these Plex calls, in this order" (the shared-reference
# cost-collapse invariant among them).
method (*Plex).CallNames is used in test only

# plexclient.NewFromHTTP — builds a real *plex.Client aimed at an
# httptest.Server. It lives here exactly so internal/plex exports only
# plex.NewClient; 28 call sites (main_test.go, internal/users/manager_test.go)
# are in packages that cannot see an internal/plex test helper.
function NewFromHTTP is used in test only

# plexclient.Options — NewFromHTTP's named-field parameter. Test-only for the
# same structural reason as NewFromHTTP itself: it names the two HTTP clients
# so the pair cannot be passed transposed.
type Options is used in test only

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Interface satisfaction.
# Blind spot: "Some possible surprises when it comes to interfaces."
#
# EU1002 (zero references) is literally accurate — nothing calls this method on
# the fake by name — but it cannot be deleted: api.PlexReader requires
# UserFromSession (internal/api/plex.go:39) and
# internal/testsupport/fakeapi/plex.go:141 asserts
# `var _ api.PlexReadWriter = (*Plex)(nil)`, so removing it breaks compilation
# everywhere the fake is passed as a PlexReader/PlexReadWriter. Satisfying an
# interface is not a reference gopls reports, which is why the count is zero.
# Production reaches the real implementation through the same interface
# (main.go:491 -> internal/plex/history.go:22).
method (*Plex).UserFromSession is unused
